== Missa Præsanctificatorum | The Mass of the Presanctified
R: Postquam Prior tradiderit Crucem Sacristæ, ipse et ministri cum duobus Presbyteris, qui cantaverunt Versus, in sacristiam revertantur, ubi duo Presbyteri se vestibus sacris exuent : Prior vero, et ministri se calceent : deinde lotis manibus, Prior casulam assumat, et præcedentibus Thuriferario, et Cruciferario, Ceroferariis, ac Subdiacono cum calice, et Diacono cum Missali, ad altare accedat, sistensque infra gradus ad medium altaris, facta profunda inclinatione, dicat : *In nómine Patris, etc. Confitémini, etc. Confíteor, etc.* Et Diaconus explicet corporale super altare, more solito, juxta illud ponens purificatorium; calix autem a Subdiacono delatus, in altari extra corporale a parte Evangelii reponatur. Interim curet Sacrista, ut baldachinum, sub quo sanctissimum Christi Corpus portandum est, præparetur, et distribuantur cerei, denturque Fratribus, et nobilioribus sæcularibus.
 = When the Prior has handed the Cross to the Sacristan, he and the ministers, with the two priests who sang the Verses, return to the sacristy, where the two priests put off the sacred vestments; the Prior and the ministers put on their shoes; then, having washed his hands, the Prior puts on the chasuble and, preceded by the thurifer, the cross-bearer, the candle-bearers, the Subdeacon with the chalice and the Deacon with the Missal, goes to the altar and, standing below the steps at the middle of the altar and bowing profoundly, says: *In nómine Patris, etc. Confitémini, etc. Confíteor, etc.* The Deacon spreads the corporal on the altar in the usual way, placing the purificator beside it; the chalice brought by the Subdeacon is set on the altar outside the corporal on the Gospel side. Meanwhile the Sacristan sees that the canopy under which the most holy Body of Christ is to be carried is made ready, and that candles are distributed and given to the brethren and to the more distinguished laymen.
R: Deinde cum silentio ad locum, ubi depositum fuit sanctissimum Sacramentum, procedatur hoc ordine : Thuriferario præcedente cum eo, qui Crucem e sepulcro delaturus est, et duobus Acolythis cum luminaribus subsequentibus, deinde incedant Fratres bini et bini (junioribus præcedentibus) et cereos non accensos deferentes : postea Prior cum casula, manus tenens junctas ante pectus, et habens a dexteris Diaconum, et a sinistris Subdiaconum. Hos subsequantur sæculares cum cereis etiam non accensis. Cum ad locum sanctissimi Sacramenti perventum fuerit, Fratres cum devotione hinc et inde ante altare se collocent genuflexi. Prior etiam genuflectat cum ministris, et accipiens de manu Diaconi thuribulum, incenset sanctissimum Sacramentum. Quo facto, Diaconus et Subdiaconus velum albi coloris a Sacrista subministratum extendant super humeros Prioris, et interim accendantur cerei. Tunc Diaconus reverenter extrahens calicem cum Sacramento e loco, ubi fuerat repositus, una cum pyxide, eum Priori porrigat, et tegat extremitatibus veli, humeris illius superpositi. Deinde surgens Sacerdos, verso vultu ad Conventum, in medio altaris stando, calicem, et pyxidem cum Sacramento, teneat elevatum ante pectus, quousque, Cantore cum solemnitate intonante *Tantum ergo Sacraméntum,* Conventus totam hanc Strophen cantaverit.
 = Then in silence they go to the place where the most holy Sacrament was laid up, in this order: the thurifer going first with the one who is to carry the Cross from the sepulchre, followed by two Acolytes with lights; then the brethren walk two by two (the juniors first) carrying unlit candles; then the Prior in the chasuble, his hands joined before his breast, with the Deacon on his right and the Subdeacon on his left. The laymen follow them, also with unlit candles. When they reach the place of the most holy Sacrament, the brethren devoutly kneel on either side before the altar. The Prior also kneels with the ministers and, taking the thurible from the Deacon's hand, incenses the most holy Sacrament. This done, the Deacon and Subdeacon spread over the Prior's shoulders a white veil handed them by the Sacristan, and meanwhile the candles are lit. Then the Deacon, reverently taking the chalice with the Sacrament from the place where it had been laid up, together with the pyx, hands it to the Prior and covers it with the ends of the veil laid on his shoulders. Then the Priest, rising and standing in the middle of the altar facing the Community, holds the chalice and pyx with the Sacrament raised before his breast until, the Cantor solemnly intoning *Tantum ergo Sacraméntum,* the Community has sung the whole of this stanza.
T: Tantum ergo Sacraméntum venerémur cérnui : et antíquum documéntum novo cedat rítui : præstet fides suppleméntum sénsuum deféctui.
 = Down in adoration falling, let us venerate so great a Sacrament; let the ancient rite give way to the new; let faith supply what the senses fail to grasp.
G: op-tantum-iii | Tantum ergo
R: Interim Diaconus, et Subdiaconus hinc inde ante Priorem celebrantem sint genuflexi : Thuriferarius autem in medio pariter genuflexus, verso vultu ad Sacramentum, illud reverenter incenset. Interim Cruciferarius Crucem, pridie ibidem depositam, accipiat, et discooperiat. Cantato *Tantum ergo,* cum sola illa Strophe, surgant Fratres, et incipiatur Hymnus *Vexílla Regis pródeunt,*
 = Meanwhile the Deacon and Subdeacon kneel on either side before the celebrating Prior; and the thurifer, likewise kneeling in the middle facing the Sacrament, reverently incenses it. Meanwhile the cross-bearer takes the Cross laid there the day before and uncovers it. When the *Tantum ergo* has been sung, with that one stanza only, the brethren rise, the hymn *Vexílla Regis pródeunt* is begun,
G: op-vexilla | Vexilla Regis
R: et processionaliter revertantur ad altare eo ordine, quo inde venerunt, excepto Thuriferario, qui immediate præcedet Sacerdotem, non incessu retrogrado, sed aliquantulum inflexo ad latus, sicque incedendo, Corpus Christi continuo incensabit.
 = And they return in procession to the altar in the order in which they came, except the thurifer, who goes immediately before the Priest, not walking backwards but turned a little to the side, and so walking continually incenses the Body of Christ.
R: Super sanctissimum Sacramentum efferetur baldachinum per digniores, vel Sacerdotes, vel sæculares. Cum autem venerint ad altare, manebunt omnes Fratres ordinati, hinc et inde, ad dexteram et ad sinistram altaris genuflexi, et Priore ante eos cum Sacramento transeunte, profunde inclinabunt, tenentes cereos semper accensos usque ad absolutam communionem : e quo etiam loco, sic genuflexi, respondebunt Celebranti. Cum Prior ad altare pervenerit, deponet super corporale calicem, et pyxidem cum Sacramento, et postea illud genuflexus adorabit, idemque facient Diaconus, et Subdiaconus. Deinde surgentes, Subdiaconus accipiat alium calicem pro Missa celebranda paratum, in eoque vinum, et aquam infundat (non tamen benedictam), et Diaconus de manu Subdiaconi calicem sic præparatum accipiens, illum patena coopertum offerat Sacerdoti : et Sacerdos, nullam oblationem faciens, illum in loco suo statuat super corporale. Deinde extrahat de altero calice sacratissimum Christi Corpus, illudque ante pedem calicis præparati pro Missa collocet; depositaque patena in loco suo, calicem palla cooperiat. Postmodum de manu Diaconi thuribulum accipiens, Sacramentum, et calicem tantum incenset, ante et post incensationem genuflexus adorans. Non autem incensabitur altare hinc inde, neque ipse Prior. Quo facto, Sacerdos digitos abluat, non tamen aversus a Sacramento ob ejus reverentiam, et ablutio in piscinam projiciatur. Tum rediens ad medium altaris genuflectat, et erectus, depositis manibus super altare, dicat voce mediocri in cantu feriali :
 = The canopy is carried over the most holy Sacrament by persons of rank, either priests or laymen. When they come to the altar, all the brethren remain arranged on either side, to the right and left of the altar, kneeling, and as the Prior passes before them with the Sacrament they bow profoundly, always holding their candles lit until the communion is finished; and from that place, kneeling thus, they answer the Celebrant. When the Prior reaches the altar, he sets the chalice and the pyx with the Sacrament on the corporal, and then kneeling adores it, and the Deacon and Subdeacon do likewise. Then, rising, the Subdeacon takes another chalice prepared for the Mass to be celebrated and pours wine and water into it (the water, however, not blessed), and the Deacon, receiving from the Subdeacon's hand the chalice so prepared, covered with the paten, presents it to the Priest; and the Priest, making no oblation, sets it in its place on the corporal. Then he takes the most holy Body of Christ from the other chalice and places it before the foot of the chalice prepared for the Mass; and having put the paten in its place, he covers the chalice with the pall. Afterwards, taking the thurible from the Deacon's hand, he incenses the Sacrament and the chalice only, adoring on his knees before and after the incensing. The altar is not incensed on either side, nor the Prior himself. This done, the Priest washes his fingers, but without turning his back to the Sacrament out of reverence for it, and the ablution is thrown into the piscina. Then, returning to the middle of the altar, he genuflects and, standing upright with his hands laid on the altar, says in a moderate voice in the ferial chant:
T: ^OREMUS. Præcéptis salutáribus móniti, et divína institutióne formáti, audémus dícere :
 = Let us pray. Admonished by saving precepts and taught by divine instruction, we make bold to say:
R: Hic more solito elevet manus, et prosequatur :
 = Here he raises his hands in the usual way and continues:
T: Pater noster, qui es in cælis : sanctificétur nomen tuum. Advéniat regnum tuum. Fiat volúntas tua, sicut in cælo, et in terra. Panem nostrum quotidiánum da nobis hódie. Et dimítte nobis débita nostra, sicut et nos dimíttimus debitóribus nostris. Et ne nos indúcas in tentatiónem. {℟.} Sed líbera nos a malo.
 = Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be Thy name; Thy kingdom come; Thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven. Give us this day our daily bread; and forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us. And lead us not into temptation. {℟.} But deliver us from evil.
R: Subjungat Sacerdos submisse *Amen.* Et immediate prosequatur Orationem :
 = The Priest adds in a low voice *Amen,* and at once continues the Oration:
T: ^LIBERA nos, quǽsumus, Dómine, ab ómnibus malis, prætéritis, præséntibus, et futúris; et intercedénte beáta et gloriósa semper Vírgine Dei Genitríce María, cum beátis Apóstolis tuis Petro et Paulo, atque Andréa, et ómnibus Sanctis, da propítius pacem in diébus nostris : ut ope misericórdiæ tuæ adjúti, et a peccáto simus semper líberi, et ab omni perturbatióne secúri. Per eúmdem Dóminum nostrum Jesum Christum Fílium tuum, qui tecum vivit et regnat in unitáte Spíritus Sancti Deus.
 = Deliver us, we beseech You, O Lord, from all evils, past, present and to come; and by the intercession of the blessed and glorious ever-Virgin Mary, Mother of God, with Your blessed Apostles Peter and Paul, and Andrew, and all the Saints, graciously grant peace in our days, that, aided by the help of Your mercy, we may be always free from sin and safe from all disturbance. Through the same our Lord Jesus Christ Your Son, who lives and reigns with You in the unity of the Holy Spirit, God.
R: Finita Oratione, Prior genuflectat, surgensque, dextera manu Hostiam sacram accipiat, et elevet, ut videri possit a populo, eamque dimittens super calicem, statim ipsam in tres partes dividat more solito. Tum voce mediocri intonet :
 = When the Oration is finished, the Prior genuflects and, rising, takes the sacred Host in his right hand and raises it so that it can be seen by the people; and lowering it over the chalice, at once divides it into three parts in the usual way. Then he intones in a moderate voice:
T: Per ómnia sǽcula sæculórum. {℟.} Amen.
 = World without end. {℟.} Amen.
R: Postea non dicatur *Pax Dómini,* nec dicatur *Agnus Dei,* nec pax ministris detur, sed immediate Sacerdos solitam particulam Hostiæ mittat in calicem nihil dicens, nullamque crucem cum particula Hostiæ faciens, aut supra, aut intra calicem. Postmodum prætermissa Oratione *Dómine Jesu Christe,* sumat reliquas partes Hostiæ, dicens :
 = Afterwards *Pax Dómini* is not said, nor *Agnus Dei,* nor is the pax given to the ministers; but the Priest at once puts the usual particle of the Host into the chalice, saying nothing and making no cross with the particle either over or within the chalice. Then, omitting the Oration *Dómine Jesu Christe,* he consumes the remaining parts of the Host, saying:
T: Corpus Dómini nostri Jesu Christi custódiat me in vitam ætérnam. Amen.
 = May the Body of our Lord Jesus Christ keep me unto life everlasting. Amen.
R: nullam mentionem faciens de Sanguine. Deinde sumens particulam Hostiæ, quæ est in calice, cum vino et aqua, nihil dicat. Peracta communione, ablutis digitis, ac sumpta purificatione, in medio altaris junctis manibus dicat :
 = making no mention of the Blood. Then, consuming the particle of the Host which is in the chalice with the wine and water, he says nothing. The communion finished, the fingers washed and the purification taken, he says in the middle of the altar with joined hands:
T: Quod ore súmpsimus, Dómine, pura mente capiámus : ut de Córpore Dómini nostri Jesu Christi fiat nobis remédium sempitérnum. Amen.
 = What we have taken with our mouth, O Lord, may we receive with a pure mind, that from the Body of our Lord Jesus Christ it may become for us an everlasting remedy. Amen.
R: Dumque fit hæc ablutio, exstinguantur cerei, et Fratres in chorum se recipiant pro Vesperis dicendis. Diaconus vero, complicato corporali, et in bursa posito, sumat Missale, et Subdiaconus calicem cum velo et bursa. Demum nulla dicatur Postcommunio, nec *Pláceat tibi, etc.* nullaque benedictio detur, sed facta reverentia Cruci altaris, Sacerdos cum ministris in sacristiam revertatur. Pyxis autem cum servatis particulis consecratis pro infirmis non remaneat in majori altari, sed portetur a Sacrista ad aliud sacellum remotius, debite præparatum, cum requisitis luminibus.
 = While this ablution is made, the candles are put out, and the brethren withdraw to the choir to say Vespers. The Deacon, having folded the corporal and put it in the burse, takes the Missal, and the Subdeacon the chalice with the veil and burse. Finally no Postcommunion is said, nor *Pláceat tibi, etc.,* and no blessing is given; but having made a reverence to the Cross of the altar, the Priest returns with the ministers to the sacristy. The pyx with the consecrated particles kept for the sick does not remain on the high altar, but is carried by the Sacristan to another, more remote chapel, duly prepared, with the requisite lights.
